Reading it: 'native - random' holds the fraction frozen fixed and
varies only whether the frozen conformations are correct, so it is
the co-solving signal with the quieter-neighbourhood effect divided
out. Near zero at every fraction means the rule is not reading its
neighbours' side chains and 77.3 % is a per-residue marginal.
'native - anchor' is the raw gain, which includes both effects.

The 'pinned drift' column must be ~0: it is the largest distance any
pinned residue's chi1 moved from where it was held. Anything else
means the pin leaked and the whole table is void.

